WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.043
A service cannot be defended well if
operators cannot understand what it is doing.

2
00:00:04.123 --> 00:00:06.382
Observability is not only about uptime
dashboards.

3
00:00:06.462 --> 00:00:07.489
For secure Go services,

4
00:00:07.569 --> 00:00:11.317
observability should include operational
health and security-relevant behavior so

5
00:00:11.397 --> 00:00:14.477
teams can detect problems, investigate
causes, and improve controls.

6
00:00:14.557 --> 00:00:16.303
Logs should capture meaningful events.

7
00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:19.515
Examples include authentication decisions,
authorization failures,

8
00:00:19.595 --> 00:00:22.573
validation failures, configuration
problems, dependency errors,

9
00:00:22.653 --> 00:00:25.220
administrative actions, and unusual
operational states.

10
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.738
The goal is not to log everything.

11
00:00:26.818 --> 00:00:30.412
The goal is to record events that help
explain important behavior without exposing

12
00:00:30.492 --> 00:00:31.878
sensitive data unnecessarily.

13
00:00:31.958 --> 00:00:34.063
Audit events deserve special design
attention.

14
00:00:34.143 --> 00:00:35.889
They should be structured, time-aware,

15
00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:38.895
and tied to useful context such as actor,
action, target, decision,

16
00:00:38.975 --> 00:00:40.310
and correlation identifiers.

17
00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:44.190
They should avoid secrets and sensitive
payloads while preserving enough information

18
00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:46.426
for review, investigation, and
accountability.

19
00:00:46.506 --> 00:00:49.535
Metrics and traces help teams identify
unusual latency, error rates,

20
00:00:49.615 --> 00:00:51.618
dependency failures, and resource
pressure.

21
00:00:51.698 --> 00:00:55.035
A spike in validation failures, a sudden
increase in authorization denials,

22
00:00:55.115 --> 00:00:57.990
or a dependency timing out more often can
all be important signals.

23
00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:01.356
These signals are easier to use when the
service exposes them consistently.

24
00:01:01.436 --> 00:01:05.236
Incident-ready architecture makes
investigation easier before an incident happens.

25
00:01:05.316 --> 00:01:08.037
Teams should know where logs go, how long
evidence is retained,

26
00:01:08.117 --> 00:01:11.557
which dashboards matter, which alerts are
actionable, and who owns follow-up.

27
00:01:11.637 --> 00:01:15.231
If these decisions are made only during an
incident, the service is already behind.

28
00:01:15.311 --> 00:01:17.210
Observability must also be privacy-aware.

29
00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:19.241
Evidence should be useful, but not
careless.

30
00:01:19.321 --> 00:01:21.940
Structured logging, redaction rules,
stable identifiers,

31
00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.665
and careful payload handling help teams
investigate while reducing the chance that

32
00:01:25.745 --> 00:01:28.980
monitoring systems become a second place
where sensitive data is exposed.
